Newton's Method of Successive Approximations
3.9.5 Newton's Rule
If x=x_{k} is an approximation to the solution x=\xi of f(x)=0 then the sequence
x_{k+1}=x_{k}-\frac{f(x_{0})}{f^{\prime}(x_{k})} will converge quadratically to x=\xi: (if instead the condition (2) above),
(1) _Monotonic convergence, f(x_{0})f^{\prime\prime}(x_{0}) and f'(x), f''(x) do not change sign in interval (x_{0},\xi), or
(2) _Oscillatory convergence, f(x_{0})f'(x_{0}) and f'(x), f'(x) do not change sign in interval (x_{0},x_{1}), x_{0}\leq\xi\leq x_{1}.
Newton's Method Applied to Real nth Roots
3.9.6 Given x"=N, if x is an approximate x=N^{1/n} then the sequence
x_{k+1}=\frac{1}{n}\left[\frac{N}{x_{k}^{n-1}}+(n-1)x_{k}\right] will converge quadratically to x.
If n=2, x_{k+1}=\frac{1}{2}\left(\frac{N}{x_{k}}+x_{k}\right),
If n=3, x_{k+1}=\frac{1}{3}\left(\frac{N}{x_{k}^{2}}+2x_{k}\right).
Aitken's x^{i}-Process for Acceleration of Sequence
3.9.7 If x_{k}, x_{k+1}, x_{k+2} are three successive iterations in a sequence converging with an error which approximately in geometric progression, then
\overline{x}_{k}=x_{k}-\frac{(x_{k}-x_{k+1})^{2}}{\Delta^{2}x_{k}}=\frac{x_{k}x_{k+2}-x_{k+1}^{2}}{\Delta^{2}x_{k}};\Delta^{2}x_{k}=x_{k}-2x_{k+1}+z\] is an improved estimate of x. In fact, if x_{k}=: O(x^{k}) then \overline{x}=x+O(x^{k}).

